            1. The 6 Best Grout Sealers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on the basis of manufacturer data sheets, the surfaces and wet areas each label approves, stated cure and reapplication times, and recurring patterns in owner feedback about staining and ease of use. Penetrating sealers rank ahead of coatings because they suit the widest range of grout and tile.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="grout-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Grout You Have Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Cement-based grout</strong>, sanded or unsanded, is the most common type in homes. It is a mix of Portland cement, fine aggregate and additives, and once cured it is porous. Water, oil and dirt can travel into it, which is why it darkens with time. Cement grout is the type that benefits from sealing, and nearly every product on this list is made for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Epoxy grout</strong> is a two-part resin system that cures into a dense, nonporous material. It resists stains and chemicals on its own and does not absorb sealer. Applying a penetrating sealer to epoxy grout accomplishes nothing and can leave residue. If your installer used epoxy, skip sealing entirely and keep it clean with a neutral cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Urethane and single-component premixed grouts</strong> fall in between. Many are marketed as never needing sealer because their resin binder already resists stains. Check the product name on the installer's paperwork or ask them. If the maker states no sealer is needed, believe them; applying one can interfere with the finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do not know what grout was used, try a water drop. Cement grout darkens within a minute or two when unsealed. Epoxy will not change at all, and the surface looks slightly glossy and feels plasticky. A grout line that beads water and still looks dry after ten minutes is either epoxy or cement grout with a working seal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your installation paperwork, a leftover grout bag in the garage, or the builder's finish schedule often names the exact product. It is worth five minutes to find out, because the label of the grout itself is the final word on whether sealing is required, recommended, or unnecessary, and it usually gives the wait time before sealing as well.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Penetrating Sealer vs Membrane vs Colorant
          </h2>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers, also called impregnators, soak into the grout and line its pores with water- and oil-repellent compounds. They do not change the color or texture, they let moisture vapor escape, and they do not peel. For most showers, floors and backsplashes, this is the right type. Aqua Mix, BulletProof, 511, Black Diamond and Tuff Duck are all penetrating sealers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Membrane or topical sealers form a thin film on top of the grout. They can add a sheen and initially repel water strongly, but in wet areas they tend to trap moisture behind the film, cloud over, and peel. On floors they wear unevenly along traffic paths. They make sense in a few places, like decorative wall tile, but rarely in showers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Colorants are pigmented coatings that both seal and recolor grout. They are the answer when grout is permanently stained or the wrong color, but they are a coating, not an impregnator, so adhesion depends on clean, dry, unsealed grout. Treat them as a restoration product rather than routine maintenance.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same split between breathable impregnators and film formers runs through every masonry surface. Our <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealer guide</a> covers how it plays out on driveways and patios, and the reasons for preferring breathable products outdoors are the same reasons they win in showers.
          </p>

